{"name":"Cool Jazz","hex":"#bee7e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cool jazz","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A genre of jazz associated with a light, calm sound and a West Coast aesthetic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cool_jazz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cool jazz","blocks":[{"text":"Cool jazz is a style and genre of modern jazz music inspired by bebop and big band that arose in the United States after World War II. It is characterized by relaxed tempos and a lighter tone than that used in the fast and complex bebop style. Cool jazz often employs formal arrangements and incorporates elements of classical music. Broadly, the genre refers to a number of post-war jazz styles employing a more subdued approach than that of contemporaneous jazz idioms. As Paul Tanner, Maurice Gerow, and David Megill suggest, \"the tonal sonorities of these conservative players could be compared to pastel colors, while the solos of [Dizzy] Gillespie and his followers could be compared to fiery red colors.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Dave_Brubeck_Quartet_1962.jpg/330px-Dave_Brubeck_Quartet_1962.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_jazz","wikidata":"Q327547"},{"title":"Cool (aesthetic)","blocks":[{"text":"Coolness, or being cool, is the aesthetic quality of something being compatible with currently admirable social norms of society or a group of people. Because of the varied and changing interpretation of what is considered cool, as well as its subjective nature, the word has no single meaning. For most, coolness is associated with exemplifying composure and self-control. When used in conversation, it is often as an expression of admiration or approval, and can be used when referencing both people and items of interest. As an aesthetic quality, cool was first used in African-American Vernacular English by jazz musicians in the 1940s, and from there spread into General American English. Although commonly regarded as slang, cool is widely used among disparate social groups and has endured in usage for generations."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_(aesthetic)","wikidata":"Q687900"},{"title":"Jazz","blocks":[{"text":"Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Its roots are in blues, ragtime, European harmony, African rhythmic rituals, spirituals, hymns, marches, vaudeville song, and dance music. Since the 1920s Jazz Age, it has been recognized as a major form of musical expression in both traditional and popular music. Jazz is characterized by swing and blue notes, complex chords, call and response vocals, polyrhythms and improvisation."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz","wikidata":"Q8341"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Chet_Baker_%281929%E2%80%931988%29.jpg/500px-Chet_Baker_%281929%E2%80%931988%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Chet_Baker_%281929%E2%80%931988%29.jpg/500px-Chet_Baker_%281929%E2%80%931988%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chet Baker, known as the \"Prince of Cool,\" 1983","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChet_Baker_(1929%E2%80%931988).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Yoruba-bronze-head.jpg/250px-Yoruba-bronze-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Yoruba-bronze-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Yoruba-bronze-head.jpg/250px-Yoruba-bronze-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yoruba bronze head sculpture from the city of Ife, Nigeria c. 12th century A.D","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoruba-bronze-head.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Malcolm-x.jpg/500px-Malcolm-x.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Malcolm-x.jpg/1280px-Malcolm-x.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Malcolm-x.jpg/500px-Malcolm-x.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Malcolm X \"embodied essential elements of cool\".[34]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMalcolm-x.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg/500px-Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg/1280px-Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg/500px-Buildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prof. Paul Waley considers Tokyo one of the world's \"capitals of cool\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABuildings_with_colorful_neon_street_signs_at_blue_hour%2C_Shinjuku%2C_Tokyo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wiki